# SYNOPSIS

```c
vx_status vx_system_power(vx_handle resource, uint32_t op);
```

# DESCRIPTION

`vx_system_power` does <op> to the whole machine.
The only <op> is `VX_POWER_OFF`, which turns it off.

On aarch64 the kernel makes PSCI's `SYSTEM_OFF` call, with `hvc` or `smc` as the FADT's ARM boot flags say; only the kernel can execute either.
On x86_64, and where the FADT says there is no PSCI, turning the machine off is ACPI's, which bus-acpi(8) does in user space by entering S5, and the call returns `VX_ERR_UNSUPPORTED`.

<resource> must be the root Resource, with `VX_RIGHT_MANAGE`.
Only devmgr(8) holds it, and it makes the call when bus-acpi asks, because ACPI found no way to turn the machine off (ADR-0031).
Programs ask bus-acpi, as poweroff(8) does; nothing else calls `vx_system_power`.

The call returns only if the machine is still on.
Nothing is stopped or synced first.

# EXAMPLES

devmgr, answering bus-acpi's request, from `servers/devmgr/devmgr.c`:

```c
} else if (m.kind == VX_ACPI_OFF) {
  say(VX_STR("powering off"), VX_STR(" (PSCI)"), VX_STR("\n"));
  st = vx_system_power(resource, VX_POWER_OFF);
```

# SEE ALSO

bus-acpi(8), devmgr(8), poweroff(8)

# DIAGNOSTICS

`vx_system_power` returns only on failure:

: `VX_ERR_INVALID`
  <op> is not `VX_POWER_OFF`; this is checked before <resource>.
: `VX_ERR_BAD_HANDLE`
  <resource> is not a handle to a Resource.
: `VX_ERR_ACCESS`
  The handle lacks `VX_RIGHT_MANAGE`.
: `VX_ERR_UNSUPPORTED`
  The machine is x86_64, or its FADT says it has no PSCI.
: `VX_ERR_IO`
  The firmware's `SYSTEM_OFF` returned.

# BUGS

There is no reboot: PSCI's `SYSTEM_RESET` and ACPI's reset register are a later <op>, to come with their first user.

The call is gated on the root Resource, the coarsest right there is; ADR-0031 accepts this while devmgr is its only caller.
